WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.241
C strings are arrays of characters terminated by a null character.

2
00:00:04.241 --> 00:00:13.367
That convention is powerful and simple, but it also means that string operations depend on a terminator being present within the valid bounds.

3
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:18.058
A character array that holds bytes is not automatically a valid C string.

4
00:00:18.058 --> 00:00:25.963
A buffer filled from a file, socket, device, parser, or fixed-width record may contain character data without a terminator.

5
00:00:25.963 --> 00:00:28.533
It may also contain embedded null bytes.

6
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:38.173
Treating that region as a C string without checking its bounds can cause a function to read past the intended data while searching for the terminator.

7
00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:41.643
The strlen function assumes a terminator can be found.

8
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:49.612
It does not know the capacity of the underlying buffer unless the data itself contains a null character before the boundary.

9
00:00:49.612 --> 00:00:57.452
If the data may be unterminated, use length-aware logic that receives the valid buffer length and stops within that range.

10
00:00:57.452 --> 00:01:04.971
Copying into fixed-size buffers requires space for the terminator when the destination is meant to become a C string.

11
00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:11.654
A destination with capacity N can hold at most N minus one ordinary characters plus the null terminator.

12
00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:19.623
Off-by-one mistakes around this rule are common because the content length and storage capacity are close but not identical.

13
00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:25.728
Truncation can be safer than writing past the destination, but it must be handled deliberately.

14
00:01:25.728 --> 00:01:31.383
A truncated name, path, identifier, token, or message may no longer mean the same thing.

15
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:39.288
The function should report truncation or make it clear that shortened output is an acceptable result for that specific use.

16
00:01:39.288 --> 00:01:46.099
Length-limited operations are useful only when their parameters are correct and their results are checked.

17
00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:54.454
Prefer APIs and helper functions that keep destination capacity, source length, resulting length, and terminator behavior visible.

18
00:01:54.454 --> 00:01:59.852
String safety is buffer safety plus terminator rules, not a separate magic category.
